{"type":"message","id":"ffd443cf","parentId":"aa40b583","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:57:08.633Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_CF8VK6g2ptQIKuteYqMZVzSO|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bca441a087d0b650458325d818f3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: rp-build\ndescription: Plan and implement substantial changes with RepoPrompt Context Builder. Use for unfamiliar repositories or changes spanning several files.\ncompatibility: Requires RepoPrompt MCP and Pi file tools.\n---\n\n# RepoPrompt Build\n\nUse the user's request as the task.\n\nStart with Git status. Locate the repository root before asking RepoPrompt to inspect anything.\n\nCall `repoprompt_context_builder` with a precise task description and `response_type: \"plan\"`. Review its plan and assumptions before changing files. Call `repoprompt_workspace_context` when you need the complete selected context. Ask the user only when an unresolved choice would materially change behavior or scope.\n\nImplement through Pi `edit` and `write`. The OrbStack repository mount is read-only, so never use RepoPrompt `apply_edits` or `file_actions`. Follow repository test-first rules for behavior changes. Run focused tests after each change, then run broader relevant checks.\n\nDo not improvise.\n\nUse `repoprompt_oracle_send` only for a specific question left open by Context Builder. Continue with the returned `chat_id` rather than starting an unrelated conversation.\n\nFor substantial changes, run RepoPrompt review against the final Git diff. Report changed files and commands run. Include validation results and remaining risks. Do not claim completion while required checks fail.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1789246628633}}
{"type":"message","id":"b26f7a5a","parentId":"ffd443cf","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:57:19.375Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Assessing context builder for wiring task**","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bca7415087d0868d40ba067b9adf\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"content\":[],\"encrypted_content\":\"REDACTED\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Assessing context builder for wiring task**\"}]}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_IteKZzzH9QyPRayfLeAgrKLz|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bca8d5cc87d0973cfb66b5448981","name":"repoprompt_context_builder","arguments":{"instructions":"<task>Plan next Phase 6 milestone: wire broker-owned replica authority around existing broker_replica_journal.py and newly built workspace_replica_publication.py. Implement verify, publish, admission, graceful drain, bounded export acknowledgment, and reclaim. Preserve no SQLite handle across external effects or await. Keep artifact bytes outside SQLite. Root protocol remains exactly prepare, launch, reclaim. Do not activate gates. Broker-backed Linux admission must remain fail-closed until later verified runtime wiring.</task><context>Current checkout is heavily dirty and contains uncommitted Phase 6 broker files. Relevant accepted modules include broker_replica_journal.py with stages ingest, verify, publish, admission, drain, export, reclaim; execution_broker.py; broker_runtime.py; broker_protocol.py; root_launcher.py; workspace_replica_publication.py; workspace_replica_artifact.py; workspace_replica_bundle.py; workspace_replica_object_pack.py; workspace_isolation.py; workspace_execution.py. Milestone commits through 4242985d are in current main. Need a concrete owner/transition/failure plan and smallest self-contained implementation boundary. Unknown transport, timeout, cancellation, verification, acknowledgment, effect, and SQLite commit outcomes must remain stage-specific unresolved. Drain is graceful broker-to-executor only. Missing acknowledgment or live unit blocks reclaim. No fourth root action.</context><discovery_agent-guidelines>Inspect current host checkout if available. If RepoPrompt mount is stale, state that explicitly and do not invent APIs.</discovery_agent-guidelines>","response_type":"plan"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":670,"output":378,"cacheRead":115072,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":44,"totalTokens":116120,"cost":{"input":0.00335,"output":0.011340000000000001,"cacheRead":0.057536,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.072226}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1789246628644,"responseId":"resp_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bca5211087d0bce9d12ea2c6e0ea","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
{"type":"message","id":"9a09a539","parentId":"b26f7a5a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:58:40.979Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IteKZzzH9QyPRayfLeAgrKLz|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bca8d5cc87d0973cfb66b5448981","toolName":"repoprompt_context_builder","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"backend\":\"headless\",\"chat_id\":\"13DA0FE2-01E7-45DC-92FF-71B0D5AB9524\",\"response\":\"The mounted checkout is stale: none of the named modules or commit `4242985d` exists in the visible repositories under `/workspace`. I therefore did not modify files or invent concrete APIs/schema fields.\\n\\n## Recommended milestone boundary\\n\\nAdd one broker-owned replica lifecycle coordinator around the existing journal and publication components. It should implement all existing journal stages, but stop short of enabling Linux admission or changing the privileged protocol.\\n\\nIn scope:\\n\\n- Recovery and orchestration for `verify → publish → admission → drain → export → reclaim`\\n- Durable, stage-specific operation identity and unresolved outcomes\\n- Graceful broker-to-executor drain\\n- Bounded export acknowledgment\\n- Reclaim eligibility requiring both export acknowledgment and absence of a live unit\\n- Crash/restart tests at every SQLite/effect boundary\\n\\nExplicitly out of scope:\\n\\n- Activating gates\\n- Allowing broker-backed Linux admission\\n- Adding a root protocol action\\n- Forced termination through “drain”\\n- Persisting artifact bytes in SQLite\\n- Choosing a new transport or changing existing accepted module APIs\\n- Automatically treating an unknown external outcome as success or failure\\n\\n## Ownership\\n\\n| Concern | Sole authority |\\n|---|---|\\n| Lifecycle stage and unresolved status | `broker_replica_journal.py` |\\n| Lifecycle decisions and sequencing | New broker-side coordinator |\\n| Artifact verification | Existing artifact/bundle/object-pack components |\\n| Published workspace materialization | `workspace_replica_publication.py` |\\n| Artifact bytes | Files/object storage outside SQLite |\\n| Graceful drain execution | Executor, requested by broker |\\n| Export production | Executor/workspace execution layer |\\n| Export acknowledgment and reclaim eligibility | Broker |\\n| Unit liveness observation | Existing broker/runtime integration |\\n| Privileged operations | Root launcher, using only `prepare`, `launch`, `reclaim` |\\n| Linux admission | Fail-closed placeholder pending later verified runtime wiring |\\n\\nThe journal records authority; publication and runtime components perform effects. They must not independently advance lifecycle state.\\n\\n## Transition plan\\n\\n1. **Ingest → Verify**\\n\\n   The broker claims a verification operation durably, commits, closes SQLite, performs verification, then opens a fresh transaction to record the result.\\n\\n   Verification must cover the accepted artifact identity and integrity constraints already provided by the artifact, bundle, and object-pack modules. Failure is terminal for that candidate; indeterminate verification remains unresolved in `verify`.\\n\\n2. **Verify → Publish**\\n\\n   Only a conclusively verified candidate may be published. The journal records publication intent and a stable operation identity before any filesystem/publication effect.\\n\\n   `workspace_replica_publication.py` performs the external effect with artifact bytes remaining outside SQLite. The broker then records the observed publication identity. If the effect may have happened but its result or SQLite commit is unknown, `publish` remains unresolved and recovery reconciles by identity; it must not blindly republish.\\n\\n3. **Publish → Admission**\\n\\n   Admission requires a conclusively verified, successfully published replica whose identities still match the journal.\\n\\n   Implement the admission decision and its durable audit result, but keep broker-backed Linux admission fail-closed. A valid publication must not become runnable until the later runtime-wiring milestone verifies and activates that path. No gate is enabled here.\\n\\n4. **Admission → Drain**\\n\\n   Drain is solely a graceful broker-to-executor request. Record intent, release SQLite, send the request, then record an explicit executor response.\\n\\n   Timeout, cancellation, transport ambiguity, or uncertain delivery remains an unresolved `drain`; none may be converted into forced termination or presumed completion.\\n\\n5. **Drain → Export**\\n\\n   Once graceful drain is conclusively accepted/completed under the existing contract, request or observe export using a durable operation identity.\\n\\n   The acknowledgment wait must have a configured finite bound. A timeout or missing acknowledgment leaves `export` unresolved. Export artifact bytes stay outside SQLite; SQLite stores only identity, metadata, and acknowledgment evidence.\\n\\n6. **Export → Reclaim**\\n\\n   Reclaim is eligible only when:\\n\\n   - the expected export has an explicit matching acknowledgment; and\\n   - the broker has conclusive evidence that no live unit remains.\\n\\n   Both conditions must be checked again immediately before invoking root `reclaim`. Record reclaim intent and close SQLite before the call. An unknown root-call or commit outcome remains unresolved in `reclaim` and must be reconciled before retry.\\n\\n## External-effect discipline\\n\\nEvery stage should use this shape:\\n\\n```text\\nSQLite transaction:\\n    validate predecessor\\n    claim stable operation identity\\n    record intent\\ncommit and close handle\\n\\nexternal effect or await\\n\\nnew SQLite transaction:\\n    re-read operation and stage\\n    record conclusive result, or stage-specific unresolved outcome\\ncommit and close handle\\n```\\n\\nNo cursor, connection, transaction, ORM/session wrapper, or lazy iterator may survive across:\\n\\n- transport calls\\n- filesystem publication\\n- verifier execution\\n- executor communication\\n- acknowledgment waits\\n- root launcher calls\\n- any `await`\\n\\nIf an SQLite commit result is itself unknown, recovery must reopen the journal and inspect the stable operation identity. If durable state still cannot be proven, preserve that stage’s unresolved state rather than issuing the effect again.\\n\\n## Failure classification\\n\\nDo not introduce a generic “failed/unknown” bucket that loses stage provenance.\\n\\n| Failure source | Required outcome |\\n|---|---|\\n| Conclusive verification rejection | Failed in `verify` |\\n| Verification crash/indeterminate result | Unresolved in `verify` |\\n| Publication effect or commit ambiguity | Unresolved in `publish` |\\n| Admission transport/policy ambiguity | Unresolved in `admission` |\\n| Drain timeout/cancellation/delivery ambiguity | Unresolved in `drain` |\\n| Missing or mismatched export acknowledgment | Unresolved in `export`; reclaim blocked |\\n| Live or indeterminately live unit | Reclaim blocked |\\n| Root reclaim or post-effect commit ambiguity | Unresolved in `reclaim` |\\n\\nCancellation should stop local waiting where safe, but must not rewrite an uncertain external outcome as “cancelled before effect.”\\n\\n## Smallest self-contained implementation\\n\\nThe smallest useful implementation is:\\n\\n- One broker coordinator using the journal as its only lifecycle authority\\n- Thin adapters to the already accepted verifier, publication, runtime/executor, and root-launcher surfaces\\n- Durable per-stage operation identities using whatever mechanism the journal already provides\\n- Recovery that resumes/reconciles exactly one unresolved stage\\n- Admission recording with an unconditional fail-closed Linux result\\n- No protocol, gate, or schema expansion unless the current journal demonstrably lacks the data needed to distinguish operation identity and stage-specific uncertainty\\n\\nSuggested completion tests:\\n\\n- Crash before and after every intent commit, external effect, and result commit\\n- Assert no SQLite handle exists while a fake external effect is running\\n- Publication reconciliation does not duplicate bytes/effects\\n- Drain timeout never calls reclaim\\n- Missing, late, duplicate, or mismatched acknowledgment blocks reclaim\\n- A live or unknown-live unit blocks reclaim\\n- Reclaim uses only the existing root `reclaim` action\\n- Linux broker admission remains denied even after successful verification/publication\\n- Restart preserves the exact unresolved stage and does not infer success\\n\\nImplementation against concrete APIs should wait until the current Phase 6 checkout is mounted.\"}"}],"details":{"server":"repoprompt","tool":"context_builder"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1789246720979}}
{"type":"message","id":"a57209d1","parentId":"9a09a539","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:58:46.612Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Listing relevant agent modules**","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd039d2c87d09d23de716b4debe5\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"content\":[],\"encrypted_content\":\"REDACTED\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Listing relevant agent modules**\"}]}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_YwudLPWoJNfSLIpmpFaSUMis|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd050b0c87d0929f4ba4a1bed097","name":"subagent","arguments":{"action":"list"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":2159,"output":49,"cacheRead":115584,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":29,"totalTokens":117792,"cost":{"input":0.010795,"output":0.00147,"cacheRead":0.057791999999999996,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.070057}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1789246720994,"responseId":"resp_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd01937887d0a5d80a0478e6e51d","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
{"type":"message","id":"a18d694e","parentId":"a57209d1","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:58:47.129Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_YwudLPWoJNfSLIpmpFaSUMis|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd050b0c87d0929f4ba4a1bed097","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Executable agents:\n- delegate (builtin): Lightweight subagent that inherits the parent model with no default reads\n- final-review (user, aliases: production-final-review): Sol Max independent design and correctness review with a remediation plan.\n- oracle (builtin, context: fork, aliases: advisor): High-context decision-consistency oracle that protects inherited state and prevents drift\n- planner (user): Read-only Sol planning for parent-approved implementation tasks.\n- qwen-implementer (user): Local Qwen implementation worker for approved file-scoped task packets\n- researcher (builtin): Autonomous web researcher — searches, evaluates, and synthesizes a focused research brief\n- reviewer (user): Read-only local Qwen reviewer for optional mechanical pre-review.\n- scout (user): Fast read-only repository reconnaissance using local Qwen.\n- test-writer (user): Local Qwen test writer for explicit behavior through an existing test interface.\n- worker (user): Default GLM High implementation worker for settled behavior and architecture.\n- worker-advanced (user): GLM Max worker for approved invariants across interacting states and failure paths.\n- worker-expert (user): Sol xhigh diagnosis and expert implementation for uncertain causes or novel algorithms.\n- worker-simple (user): Local Qwen implementation for fully specified mechanical changes.\n\nChains:\n- (none)"}],"details":{"mode":"management","results":[]},"isError":false,"timestamp":1789246727129}}
{"type":"message","id":"caaf353a","parentId":"a18d694e","timestamp":"2026-09-12T20:59:00.334Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Planning parallel scout tasks**","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd0c866887d0a9cabe18acde3b97\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"content\":[],\"encrypted_content\":\"REDACTED\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Planning parallel scout tasks**\"}]}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_emZnescRBfpWv05nzY40UMeR|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd0e0a4087d0b305f5dc863c25cc","name":"subagent","arguments":{"workflowScript":"const tasks=[\n {key:'journal',agent:'scout',task:'Read broker_replica_journal.py and tests. Report exact public API, stage transitions, receipts, replay/recovery behavior, and gaps for a lifecycle coordinator. No edits.'},\n {key:'broker',agent:'scout',task:'Read execution_broker.py, broker_runtime.py, broker_protocol.py, execution_daemons.py, and tests. Report exact request flow, startup ownership, transport failure classes, and extension points for replica coordinator. No edits.'},\n {key:'publication',agent:'scout',task:'Read workspace_replica_publication.py plus artifact/bundle/object-pack modules. Report exact callable contract, receipts, failure classes, byte ownership, and coordinator integration constraints. No edits.'},\n {key:'execution',agent:'scout',task:'Read workspace_isolation.py, workspace_execution.py, workspace_admission.py, root_launcher.py, and relevant tests. Report exact admission, liveness, drain, export, reclaim contracts and current fail-closed behavior. No edits.'},\n {key:'testing',agent:'scout',task:'Map current tests for broker replica lifecycle, root replica lifecycle, execution recovery, sidecar admission/terminal, and publication. Propose smallest test-first coordinator boundary using existing patterns. No edits.'}\n]; return runs.all(tasks);","async":false,"cwd":"/Users/user/projects/yinshi","timeoutMs":1200000,"turnBudget":{"maxTurns":5,"graceTurns":1},"toolBudget":{"soft":18,"hard":28,"block":["read","grep","find","ls"]},"chatProgress":"live-card"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":436,"output":418,"cacheRead":117632,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":52,"totalTokens":118486,"cost":{"input":0.00218,"output":0.01254,"cacheRead":0.058816,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.073536}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1789246727157,"responseId":"resp_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd07cbb887d0a326026bb85c4e2f","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
{"type":"message","id":"b1ef3c46","parentId":"caaf353a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T21:05:34.492Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_emZnescRBfpWv05nzY40UMeR|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5bd0e0a4087d0b305f5dc863c25cc","toolName":"subagent","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Workflow completed.\n\nReturn:\n[\n  {\n    \"key\": \"journal\",\n    \"ok\": true,\n    \"runId\": \"061851b5\",\n    \"output\": \"Turn budget wrap-up was requested after 5 assistant turns (soft limit 5, grace 1). Process-mode live steering is unavailable, so the child was warned at launch to wrap up by this budget. Output may be partial.\\n\\n## Findings Report\\n\\n### Source File\\n`backend/src/yinshi/services/broker_replica_journal.py` (1,947 lines, ~50KB)\\n\\n### Test File\\n`backend/tests/test_broker_repica_journal.py` (21 tests, all passing)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Public API — `BrokerReplicaJournal`\\n\\n**Constructor**\\n- `__init__(self, path: Path, *, application_id: str) -> None` — opens/creates SQLite at `path`, validates schema on init.\\n\\n**Properties**\\n- `path: Path` — read-only path to the SQLite file.\\n\\n**Lifecycle State Queries**\\n- `status(self, request: BrokerRequest) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — returns `absent`, `accepted`, `claimed`, `in_flight`, `completed`, `rejected`, or `unresolved`.\\n- `incomplete_lifecycles(self) -> tuple[IncompleteReplicaLifecycle, ...]` — lists non-terminal claimed lifecycles for restart recovery.\\n\\n**Write Operations (idempotent where noted)**\\n- `accept(self, request, request_frame, authority) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — idempotent on (db_incarnation, op_id, type).\\n- `claim_authority(self, request, *, owner_token, broker_incarnation) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — idempotent on owner_token.\\n- `begin_stage(self, request, *, owner_token, stage) -> ReplicaJournalPosition` — idempotent on (stage).\\n- `complete_ingest/complete_verify/complete_publish/complete_admission/complete_drain/complete_export/complete_reclaim` — each takes request, owner_token, receipt, and optional response_frame. Idempotent on exact match.\\n- `record_rejected(self, request, *, owner_token, stage, status, receipt, response_frame) -> ReplicaJournalPosition`\\n- `record_unresolved(self, request, *, owner_token, stage, reason, response_frame) -> ReplicaJournalPosition`\\n\\n**Data Classes (immutable receipt types)**\\n- `ReplicaAuthority` (physical_target_id, replica_generation, execution_owner_id)\\n- `ArtifactReference` (artifact_id, sha256, byte_length)\\n- `IngestReceipt`, `VerifyReceipt`, `PublishReceipt`, `AdmissionReceipt`, `DrainReceipt`, `ExportReceipt`, `ReclaimReceipt`, `RejectedReceipt`\\n- `ReplicaJournalPosition` (journal_id, sequence, append_receipt_id)\\n- `ReplicaJournalDecision` (state, stage, response_frame, owner_broker_incarnation)\\n- `IncompleteReplicaLifecycle` (request_frame, owner_token, owner_broker_incarnation, stage, stage_started, reason)\\n\\n**Errors**\\n- `ReplicaJournalError` (base)\\n- `ReplicaJournalSyncError` (malformed/uncertain durable state)\\n- `ReplicaJournalCommitAbsent` (commit absent after fresh connection check)\\n- `ReplicaJournalConflictError` (conflicts with immutable event)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Stage Transitions\\n\\n**Order**: `ingest → verify → publish → admission → drain → export → reclaim`\\n\\nDefined in `_STAGES = (\\\"ingest\\\", \\\"verify\\\", \\\"publish\\\", \\\"admission\\\", \\\"drain\\\", \\\"export\\\", \\\"reclaim\\\")`\\n\\n**Lifecycle**:\\n1. `accept()` — creates event `accepted`. State: `accepted`.\\n2. `claim_authority()` — creates event `authority_claimed`. State: `claimed`.\\n3. `begin_stage(stage)` — creates event `stage_started`. State: `in_flight`.\\n4a. `complete_*()` — creates event `stage_outcome`. State: `completed` for this stage. Only `reclaim` is terminal.\\n4b. `record_rejected()` — creates event `stage_rejected`. State: `rejected`. Terminal.\\n4c. `record_unresolved()` — creates event `stage_unresolved`. State: `unresolved`. Terminal.\\n\\n**Constraints** (enforced by SQLite triggers + replay):\\n- `accepted` must exist before any other event type.\\n- `authority_claimed` must exist before stage events (verified via owner_token).\\n- `stage_started` must precede `stage_outcome`/`stage_rejected`/`stage_unresolved` for the same stage.\\n- Stages must proceed in fixed order — no skipping or reordering.\\n- Terminal states (`rejected`, `unresolved`, or `reclaim`-outcome) are immutable.\\n- Events are never updatable/deletable (SQLite triggers).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Receipts\\n\\nEach stage has a typed receipt dataclass. The `_receipt_json()` function serializes to canonical JSON. Max 2,048 bytes per receipt.\\n\\n| Stage | Receipt Fields |\\n|-------|---------------|\\n| ingest | receipt_id, bundle (artifact_id, sha256, byte_length), worktree (nullable) |\\n| verify | receipt_id, bundle_sha256, worktree_sha256, source_state_sha256, reconciliation_fingerprint |\\n| publish | receipt_id, physical_target_id, replica_generation, publication_marker_sha256, synchronization_receipt_id |\\n| admission | receipt_id, execution_owner_id, runtime_unit_id, session_socket_sha256 |\\n| drain | drain_ack_receipt_id, quiescence_receipt_id |\\n| export | export_receipt_id, bundle, worktree (nullable) |\\n| reclaim | reclaim_receipt_id |\\n\\n**RejectedReceipt** — single `receipt_id` field.\\n\\nReceipt binding validation (`_validate_receipt_binding`) cross-checks receipt fields against the original request payload and authority. This prevents replay of receipts from different requests.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Replay/Recovery Behavior\\n\\n**On Open**: `_validate()` runs `PRAGMA integrity_check`, re-builds expected schema from statements via in-memory DB, compares against disk. Replays all events for each (db_incarnation, op_id, request_type) group through `_replay()` which enforces:\\n- Strict ordering of event types\\n- No events after terminal state\\n- Correct stage sequencing\\n- Response frame signature and digest validation\\n- Binding consistency between frames and events\\n\\n**Restart Recovery**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` iterates all identities, replays each, and returns non-terminal claimed lifecycles with `reason=\\\"broker_restart_unknown\\\"`. Caller uses request_frame + owner_token to resume.\\n\\n**Commit-Unknown Resilience** (`_append`): If commit fails (before or after sqlite response), `_confirm_appended_event` queries by `append_receipt_id`. If present (idempotent replay), returns position. If absent and no collision, raises `ReplicaJournalCommitAbsent`. If collision, raises `ReplicaJournalConflictError`.\\n\\n**Idempotency**: All write methods check for existing matching events and return the stored `ReplicaJournalPosition` without appending again.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Gaps and Risks\\n\\n**RISK [medium]**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` returns `request_frame` for resumed lifecycles. The caller must have the original `BrokerRequest` object to call `status()` or `begin_stage()`. There is no `request_from_frame()` method on the journal — caller must reconstruct.\\n\\n**RISK [low]**: `_commit_transaction` is marked \\\"overridable fault boundary\\\" and tested via `FaultJournal`, but `_commit_transaction` only calls `database.commit()`. No rollback on failure — if the commit succeeds but `database.close()` fails, the event is persisted but no position is returned. The `_append` method handles this by falling through to `_confirm_appended_event`, but the code path through `event_id` could miss the confirmation if `database.close()` raises.\\n\\n**GAP [low]**: No explicit TTL or pruning of completed lifecycles. The journal grows unbounded.\\n\\n**GAP [low]**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` loads all identities into memory. No cursor-based pagination. Could be heavy with many operations.\\n\\n**NOTE**: `_validate_response_frame` in `record_unresolved` and `complete_reclaim` validates the response frame. However, `_complete` for non-terminal stages explicitly rejects any `response_frame` passed in. This is correct — only terminal stages emit response frames.\\n\\n**NOTE**: The `_append` method uses `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` (not `BEGIN DEFERRED`), preventing reader-writer deadlock scenarios. Good.\\n\\n**NOTE**: `PRAGMA synchronous = FULL` and `PRAGMA journal_mode = DELETE` provide strong durability at cost of performance. Acceptable for a journal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Test Coverage Summary\\n\\n21 tests, all passing. Coverage includes:\\n- Schema immutability (triggers, metadata)\\n- Full lifecycle replay from disk\\n- Transition ordering requirements\\n- Terminal state enforcement (rejected + unresolved)\\n- Restart reporting (only claimed non-terminal)\\n- Artifact byte isolation (no inline content in SQLite)\\n- Commit fault injection (before/after commit)\\n- Schema/history corruption rejection\\n- Frame/sequence corruption rejection\\n- Inline content injection blocking\\n- Protocol binding enforcement\\n- Commit-absence via stage unique key\\n- Concurrent exact-accept race\\n- Model bounds validation\\n- Stage-specific unresolved reasons\\n\\nMissing: concurrent writes at different stages, partial failure mid-lifecycle recovery from disk, large receipt handling near byte limit.\",\n    \"artifactPaths\": [\n      \"/Users/user/.pi/agent/sessions/--Users-user-projects-yinshi--/2026-09-04T04-44-52-550Z_01a06abb-dfc5-7c65-82bb-7c5f2081ee64/061851b5/run-0/session.jsonl\"\n    ],\n    \"results\": [\n      {\n        \"index\": 0,\n        \"agent\": \"scout\",\n        \"task\": \"Read broker_replica_journal.py and tests. Report exact public API, stage transitions, receipts, replay/recovery behavior, and gaps for a lifecycle coordinator. No edits.\",\n        \"routingContract\": {\n          \"version\": 1,\n          \"agent\": \"scout\",\n          \"model\": \"local-pilocal/qwen36-35b-a3b-iq4xs-mtp3-turbo3\",\n          \"thinking\": \"high\",\n          \"policyDigest\": \"8590d2fc0420152aad3f867aba1c18a56b3632f2d90e01d30d3f04676cd46ac5\"\n        },\n        \"launchContractDigest\": \"1d5de134eaf225505c68836b7a3e7c890183db4f587c4dbf1b6dccaee427d4f5\",\n        \"launchResolvedExtensions\": {\n          \"version\": 1,\n          \"source\": \"launch-resolved\",\n          \"disableAmbientExtensions\": false,\n          \"runtime\": [\n            \"sha256:cb00ccf63b7cf9f4\"\n          ],\n          \"configured\": [],\n          \"effective\": [\n            \"sha256:cb00ccf63b7cf9f4\"\n          ],\n          \"omitted\": {\n            \"runtime\": 0,\n            \"configured\": 0,\n            \"effective\": 0\n          }\n        },\n        \"exitCode\": 0,\n        \"outputState\": \"present\",\n        \"usage\": {\n          \"input\": 29464,\n          \"output\": 4121,\n          \"cacheRead\": 303132,\n          \"cacheWrite\": 0,\n          \"cost\": 0,\n          \"turns\": 13\n        },\n        \"model\": \"local-pilocal/qwen36-35b-a3b-iq4xs-mtp3-turbo3:high\",\n        \"thinking\": \"high\",\n        \"artifactPaths\": {\n          \"inputPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0_input.md\",\n          \"outputPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0_output.md\",\n          \"jsonlPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0.jsonl\",\n          \"transcriptPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0_transcript.jsonl\",\n          \"metadataPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0_meta.json\"\n        },\n        \"transcriptPath\": \"/Users/user/projects/yinshi/.pi-subagents/artifacts/061851b5_scout_0_transcript.jsonl\",\n        \"turnBudget\": {\n          \"maxTurns\": 5,\n          \"graceTurns\": 1,\n          \"turnCount\": 13,\n          \"outcome\": \"wrap-up-requested\",\n          \"wrapUpRequestedAtTurn\": 5\n        },\n        \"toolBudget\": {\n          \"hard\": 28,\n          \"soft\": 18,\n          \"block\": [\n            \"read\",\n            \"grep\",\n            \"find\",\n            \"ls\"\n          ],\n          \"toolCount\": 18,\n          \"outcome\": \"soft-reached\",\n          \"softReachedAt\": 18\n        },\n        \"context\": \"fresh\",\n        \"wrapUpRequested\": true,\n        \"progressSummary\": {\n          \"toolCount\": 18,\n          \"tokens\": 33585,\n          \"durationMs\": 393442\n        },\n        \"effects\": {\n          \"fileMutation\": {\n            \"status\": \"not-applicable\",\n            \"expected\": false,\n            \"attempted\": true\n          }\n        },\n        \"outputMode\": \"inline\",\n        \"finalOutput\": \"Turn budget wrap-up was requested after 5 assistant turns (soft limit 5, grace 1). Process-mode live steering is unavailable, so the child was warned at launch to wrap up by this budget. Output may be partial.\\n\\n## Findings Report\\n\\n### Source File\\n`backend/src/yinshi/services/broker_replica_journal.py` (1,947 lines, ~50KB)\\n\\n### Test File\\n`backend/tests/test_broker_repica_journal.py` (21 tests, all passing)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Public API — `BrokerReplicaJournal`\\n\\n**Constructor**\\n- `__init__(self, path: Path, *, application_id: str) -> None` — opens/creates SQLite at `path`, validates schema on init.\\n\\n**Properties**\\n- `path: Path` — read-only path to the SQLite file.\\n\\n**Lifecycle State Queries**\\n- `status(self, request: BrokerRequest) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — returns `absent`, `accepted`, `claimed`, `in_flight`, `completed`, `rejected`, or `unresolved`.\\n- `incomplete_lifecycles(self) -> tuple[IncompleteReplicaLifecycle, ...]` — lists non-terminal claimed lifecycles for restart recovery.\\n\\n**Write Operations (idempotent where noted)**\\n- `accept(self, request, request_frame, authority) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — idempotent on (db_incarnation, op_id, type).\\n- `claim_authority(self, request, *, owner_token, broker_incarnation) -> ReplicaJournalDecision` — idempotent on owner_token.\\n- `begin_stage(self, request, *, owner_token, stage) -> ReplicaJournalPosition` — idempotent on (stage).\\n- `complete_ingest/complete_verify/complete_publish/complete_admission/complete_drain/complete_export/complete_reclaim` — each takes request, owner_token, receipt, and optional response_frame. Idempotent on exact match.\\n- `record_rejected(self, request, *, owner_token, stage, status, receipt, response_frame) -> ReplicaJournalPosition`\\n- `record_unresolved(self, request, *, owner_token, stage, reason, response_frame) -> ReplicaJournalPosition`\\n\\n**Data Classes (immutable receipt types)**\\n- `ReplicaAuthority` (physical_target_id, replica_generation, execution_owner_id)\\n- `ArtifactReference` (artifact_id, sha256, byte_length)\\n- `IngestReceipt`, `VerifyReceipt`, `PublishReceipt`, `AdmissionReceipt`, `DrainReceipt`, `ExportReceipt`, `ReclaimReceipt`, `RejectedReceipt`\\n- `ReplicaJournalPosition` (journal_id, sequence, append_receipt_id)\\n- `ReplicaJournalDecision` (state, stage, response_frame, owner_broker_incarnation)\\n- `IncompleteReplicaLifecycle` (request_frame, owner_token, owner_broker_incarnation, stage, stage_started, reason)\\n\\n**Errors**\\n- `ReplicaJournalError` (base)\\n- `ReplicaJournalSyncError` (malformed/uncertain durable state)\\n- `ReplicaJournalCommitAbsent` (commit absent after fresh connection check)\\n- `ReplicaJournalConflictError` (conflicts with immutable event)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Stage Transitions\\n\\n**Order**: `ingest → verify → publish → admission → drain → export → reclaim`\\n\\nDefined in `_STAGES = (\\\"ingest\\\", \\\"verify\\\", \\\"publish\\\", \\\"admission\\\", \\\"drain\\\", \\\"export\\\", \\\"reclaim\\\")`\\n\\n**Lifecycle**:\\n1. `accept()` — creates event `accepted`. State: `accepted`.\\n2. `claim_authority()` — creates event `authority_claimed`. State: `claimed`.\\n3. `begin_stage(stage)` — creates event `stage_started`. State: `in_flight`.\\n4a. `complete_*()` — creates event `stage_outcome`. State: `completed` for this stage. Only `reclaim` is terminal.\\n4b. `record_rejected()` — creates event `stage_rejected`. State: `rejected`. Terminal.\\n4c. `record_unresolved()` — creates event `stage_unresolved`. State: `unresolved`. Terminal.\\n\\n**Constraints** (enforced by SQLite triggers + replay):\\n- `accepted` must exist before any other event type.\\n- `authority_claimed` must exist before stage events (verified via owner_token).\\n- `stage_started` must precede `stage_outcome`/`stage_rejected`/`stage_unresolved` for the same stage.\\n- Stages must proceed in fixed order — no skipping or reordering.\\n- Terminal states (`rejected`, `unresolved`, or `reclaim`-outcome) are immutable.\\n- Events are never updatable/deletable (SQLite triggers).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Receipts\\n\\nEach stage has a typed receipt dataclass. The `_receipt_json()` function serializes to canonical JSON. Max 2,048 bytes per receipt.\\n\\n| Stage | Receipt Fields |\\n|-------|---------------|\\n| ingest | receipt_id, bundle (artifact_id, sha256, byte_length), worktree (nullable) |\\n| verify | receipt_id, bundle_sha256, worktree_sha256, source_state_sha256, reconciliation_fingerprint |\\n| publish | receipt_id, physical_target_id, replica_generation, publication_marker_sha256, synchronization_receipt_id |\\n| admission | receipt_id, execution_owner_id, runtime_unit_id, session_socket_sha256 |\\n| drain | drain_ack_receipt_id, quiescence_receipt_id |\\n| export | export_receipt_id, bundle, worktree (nullable) |\\n| reclaim | reclaim_receipt_id |\\n\\n**RejectedReceipt** — single `receipt_id` field.\\n\\nReceipt binding validation (`_validate_receipt_binding`) cross-checks receipt fields against the original request payload and authority. This prevents replay of receipts from different requests.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Replay/Recovery Behavior\\n\\n**On Open**: `_validate()` runs `PRAGMA integrity_check`, re-builds expected schema from statements via in-memory DB, compares against disk. Replays all events for each (db_incarnation, op_id, request_type) group through `_replay()` which enforces:\\n- Strict ordering of event types\\n- No events after terminal state\\n- Correct stage sequencing\\n- Response frame signature and digest validation\\n- Binding consistency between frames and events\\n\\n**Restart Recovery**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` iterates all identities, replays each, and returns non-terminal claimed lifecycles with `reason=\\\"broker_restart_unknown\\\"`. Caller uses request_frame + owner_token to resume.\\n\\n**Commit-Unknown Resilience** (`_append`): If commit fails (before or after sqlite response), `_confirm_appended_event` queries by `append_receipt_id`. If present (idempotent replay), returns position. If absent and no collision, raises `ReplicaJournalCommitAbsent`. If collision, raises `ReplicaJournalConflictError`.\\n\\n**Idempotency**: All write methods check for existing matching events and return the stored `ReplicaJournalPosition` without appending again.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Gaps and Risks\\n\\n**RISK [medium]**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` returns `request_frame` for resumed lifecycles. The caller must have the original `BrokerRequest` object to call `status()` or `begin_stage()`. There is no `request_from_frame()` method on the journal — caller must reconstruct.\\n\\n**RISK [low]**: `_commit_transaction` is marked \\\"overridable fault boundary\\\" and tested via `FaultJournal`, but `_commit_transaction` only calls `database.commit()`. No rollback on failure — if the commit succeeds but `database.close()` fails, the event is persisted but no position is returned. The `_append` method handles this by falling through to `_confirm_appended_event`, but the code path through `event_id` could miss the confirmation if `database.close()` raises.\\n\\n**GAP [low]**: No explicit TTL or pruning of completed lifecycles. The journal grows unbounded.\\n\\n**GAP [low]**: `incomplete_lifecycles()` loads all identities into memory. No cursor-based pagination. Could be heavy with many operations.\\n\\n**NOTE**: `_validate_response_frame` in `record_unresolved` and `complete_reclaim` validates the response frame. However, `_complete` for non-terminal stages explicitly rejects any `response_frame` passed in. This is correct — only terminal stages emit response frames.\\n\\n**NOTE**: The `_append` method uses `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` (not `BEGIN DEFERRED`), preventing reader-writer deadlock scenarios. Good.\\n\\n**NOTE**: `PRAGMA synchronous = FULL` and `PRAGMA journal_mode = DELETE` provide strong durability at cost of performance. Acceptable for a journal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. Test Coverage Summary\\n\\n21 tests, all passing. Coverage includes:\\n- Schema immutability (triggers, metadata)\\n- Full lifecycle replay from disk\\n- Transition ordering requirements\\n- Terminal state enforcement (rejected + unresolved)\\n- Restart reporting (only claimed non-terminal)\\n- Artifact byte isolation (no inline content in SQLite)\\n- Commit fault injection (before/after commit)\\n- Schema/history corruption rejection\\n- Frame/sequence corruption rejection\\n- Inline content injection blocking\\n- Protocol binding enforcement\\n- Commit-absence via stage unique key\\n- Concurrent exact-accept race\\n- Model bounds validation\\n- Stage-specific unresolved reasons\\n\\nMissing: concurrent writes at different stages, partial failure mid-lifecycle recovery from disk, large receipt handling near byte limit.\",\n        \"controlEvents\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"needs_attention\",\n            \"to\": \"needs_attention\",\n            \"ts\": 1789246974441,\n            \"runId\": \"061851b5\",\n            \"agent\": \"scout\",\n            \"index\": 0,\n            \"message\": \"scout needs attention (no observed activity for 60s)\",\n            \"reason\": \"idle\",\n            \"turns\": 5,\n            \"tokens\": 28270,\n            \"toolCoun