rdinator(replica, _effects(trace, {\"ingest\": ingest})).recover_incomplete()\n     assert trace == [(\"reconcile\", \"ingest\")]\n     assert _result(replica.status(request).response_frame)[\"code\"] == \"verification_unknown\"\n","firstChangedLine":336},"isError":false,"timestamp":1789262938352}}
{"type":"message","id":"ed1fea56","parentId":"e17b0c7a","timestamp":"2026-09-13T01:29:02.239Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Planning final design review**","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5fc5c54a887d0ab3ed6c496e4c0a8\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"content\":[],\"encrypted_content\":\"REDACTED\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Planning final design review**\"}]}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_jutqicFtZcp0SErUWeU8ttbl|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5fc5d995c87d09f6b798a619eaf4e","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/Users/user/.pi/agent/skills/software-design/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":461,"output":73,"cacheRead":98560,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":40,"totalTokens":99094,"cost":{"input":0.002305,"output":0.00219,"cacheRead":0.04928,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.053774999999999996}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1789262938359,"responseId":"resp_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5fc5ae68887d09ebbb058546b3c39","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
{"type":"message","id":"fbf3949f","parentId":"ed1fea56","timestamp":"2026-09-13T01:29:02.258Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_jutqicFtZcp0SErUWeU8ttbl|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa5fc5d995c87d09f6b798a619eaf4e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: software-design\ndescription: Applies principles from \"A Philosophy of Software Design\" to architecture, module and API boundaries, and consequential refactors. Use for architecture planning, module or API design, design review, or diagnosing shallow modules, information leakage, and pass-through layers.\n---\n\n# Software Design\n\nApply these principles when architecture or abstraction quality is a material part of the task.\n\n## Two Modes\n\n### Writing New Code\n\nApply these principles in order of impact.\n\n#### Think strategically\n\nInvest in design instead of stopping when the code first works. Produce a clean design that works, and spend 10–20% of the effort on design improvements.\n\n#### Create deep modules\n\nEach module should provide powerful functionality behind a simple interface. A few deep methods beat many shallow ones. Ask whether the interface is simpler than the implementation.\n\n#### Hide information\n\nEach module should encapsulate design decisions that remain invisible through its interface. If one decision appears in several modules, reorganize the code until that decision has one home.\n\n#### Make interfaces general-purpose\n\nDesign an interface broad enough to support current uses while keeping its functionality focused. Ask for the simplest interface that covers every current need.\n\n#### Give each layer a different abstraction\n\nAdjacent layers should operate at different abstraction levels. Remove pass-through methods that delegate with the same signature.\n\n#### Pull complexity downwards\n\nHandle complexity inside the module instead of pushing it to callers. A simple interface matters more than a simple implementation.\n\n#### Define errors out of existence\n\nRedefine APIs so avoidable error conditions cannot arise. Mask low-level exceptions and consolidate their handling. Reserve crashes for unrecoverable failures.\n\n#### Design it twice\n\nConsider at least two radically different approaches before committing. Compare their strengths and costs. Apply this comparison to interfaces and implementations, then consider the broader system decomposition.\n\n#### Name precisely\n\nNames should create a clear mental image of an entity's purpose. Avoid vague choices such as `data`, `result`, `info`, or `tmp`. Name booleans with predicates such as `isConnected` instead of a state label such as `status`.\n\n#### Be consistent\n\nUse the same name for the same purpose everywhere. Reuse established coding patterns and conventions. Consistency reduces the reader's cognitive work.\n\n#### Make code obvious\n\nCode is obvious when a reader's first guess about its behaviour is correct. Choose precise names and follow established patterns. Use whitespace to expose structure, and comment only where the code cannot explain itself.\n\n#### Write comments first\n\nWrite interface comments before implementation. A method that needs a long descriptive comment has a complex interface and should be redesigned. Comments should preserve information that code cannot represent.\n\n### Reviewing Existing Code\n\nScan for these red flags in the listed order.\n\n#### Shallow modules\n\nLook for interfaces as complex as their implementations, many trivial methods, or classes with little functionality.\n\n#### Information leakage\n\nFind design decisions repeated across modules. A change to one detail should not require coordinated edits across several classes.\n\n#### Temporal decomposition\n\nCheck for code organized around operation order instead of information hiding, such as separate read and write classes for one format.\n\n#### Pass-through methods\n\nFlag methods that only call another method with the same signature, especially when delegation accounts for half or more of a class's methods.\n\n#### Overexposure\n\nA common operation should not force callers to understand rare features. Default information that callers should not have to provide.\n\n#### Conjoined methods\n\nFlag a method when understanding it requires reading another method's implementation.\n\n#### Repetition\n\nFind non-trivial code patterns duplicated in several places.\n\n#### Vague or hard-to-pick names\n\nFlag names broad enough to describe many entities. Difficulty finding a precise name often means the entity has too many responsibilities.\n\n#### Comment problems\n\nFind comments that restate the code or expose implementation details in interface documentation. Long, complicated descriptions may reveal a poor abstraction.\n\n#### Special-general mixture\n\nKeep specialized use-case code out of a general-purpose mechanism.\n\n#### Nonobvious code\n\nFlag behaviour that a quick reading cannot reveal, including surprising side effects.\n\n#### Too many exceptions\n\nLook for exceptions the caller cannot handle and error paths longer than the normal path. Redefine operations when doing so can eliminate error conditions.\n\nFor the full checklist, read `references/red-flags.md`.\n\n## References\n\n`references/principles.md` covers all 15 design principles. It includes examples and techniques, along with the reasoning behind each principle. Read it when applying a specific principle or when deeper context is needed.\n\n`references/red-flags.md` organizes every red flag by category. Read it during code review to check systematically for design problems.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1789262942258}}
