src/yinshi/services/broker_artifact_store.py` — `BrokerArtifactStore.receive(manifest, reader)` streams **raw bytes after the signed frame** from an `asyncio.StreamReader` (3 fixed files, 512 MiB/role, 1 GiB/set, 120 s transfer / 30 s idle timeouts), atomic no-replace publish, quarantine + `reconcile_attempts`; requires `expected_uid == os.geteuid()` (broker UID 999).\n- No production code creates signed requests (`create_signed_request` has zero non-test callers); the name `replica.artifacts.upload` appears **nowhere** in the repository — it is a new request type.\n- Tests: `test_broker_foundation.py` (unsupported type rejected), `test_execution_daemons.py` (one peer-bound exchange; strictly nested deadlines `30 < 35 < 40 < 120`), and the 97 replica journal/lifecycle/artifact-store tests all pass (see commands).\n\n## 2. Proposed dispatch design (no code changed)\n\n1. **Single front door, per-type pipelines.** Keep the daemon peer-UID check in `_exchange_broker` exactly as is. Add one dispatcher that calls `parse_signed_request` once (signature is over the whole frame, so routing necessarily follows verification), checks both incarnations, then routes on `request.request_type`:\n   - `executor.launch` → `BrokerService.handle(frame, peer_uid=…)` **unchanged** (it re-verifies; zero behavioral delta, forbidden-field scan stays scoped here — note `_FORBIDDEN_LAUNCH_FIELDS` contains `\"replica\"`, so it must never be applied to replica payloads).\n   - `replica.lifecycle` → `BrokerReplicaLifecycleCoordinator.run(request, frame, authority)`.\n   - `replica.artifacts.upload` → new upload pipeline: build `ReplicaArtifactManifest` from the signed payload, then `BrokerArtifactStore.receive(manifest, reader)`.\n   - anything else → today's `BrokerProtocolError(\"broker request type is not supported\")` (drop connection, no response — preserves the fail-closed precedent; one existing test asserts this).\n2. **Authentication ownership.** Kernel peer UID stays daemon-owned (out