{"type":"message","id":"c28988f5","parentId":"569a4d6d","timestamp":"2026-09-13T11:20:04.242Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_jDzE1gqYp4w5TWBNxQ3iRI9B|fc_0212e7de83dbbd32016aa686e2afdc87d0be1172bffa978bd9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":" M backend/src/yinshi/services/broker_artifact_store.py\n?? backend/src/yinshi/broker_artifact_stdio_relay.py\n?? backend/tests/test_broker_artifact_stdio_relay.py\n?? backend/tests/test_broker_artifact_transport.py\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789297411025,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:03:31.025Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Task: You are a delegated subagent running from a fork of the parent session. Treat the inherited conversation as reference-only context, not a live thread to continue. Do not continue or answer prior messages as if they are waiting for a reply. Your sole job is to execute the task below and return a focused result for that task using your tools.\\n\\nTask:\\nFix all three findings from final review 5dcf482a in current shared checkout. Do not commit. 1) Relay must concurrently watch signed broker response while streaming body. A complete valid framed response wins over later upload-side BrokenPipe/connection errors. Cancel and drain upload safely once response arrives. If upload finishes first, await response. Add integrated large remaining body early metadata rejection test. 2) BrokerArtifactStore concurrent exact retries: when no-replace publication loses, quarantine losing pending attempt then inspect the winning final against exact manifest. Exact match returns deterministic receipt, conflicting final remains rejection/unresolved per confirmed state. Add synchronized concurrent exact and conflicting manifest tests. Preserve root lock/capacity, cancellation, directory sync, and no mutable deletion. 3) CLI control-frame read must be under one absolute relay timeout. Build async pipe reader first, read length/control and remaining artifacts through the same reader so buffering is preserved. Avoid blocking sys.stdin reads before timeout. Add partial-prefix and partial-control-frame stall tests at the appropriate async boundary. Keep existing one-frame relay untouched. Review current source and tests first. Run focused tests, Ruff, format, strict mypy, py_compile, diff check. If TDD Guard blocks, return exact edit packet. No semicolons.\\n\\n## Acceptance Contract\\nAcceptance level: checked\\nCompletion is not accepted from prose alone. End with a structured acceptance report.\\n\\nCriteria:\\n- criterion-1: Implement the requested change without widening scope\\n\\nRequired evidence: changed-files, tests-added, commands-run, residual-risks, no-staged-files, validation-output\\n\\nFinish with a fenced JSON block tagged `acceptance-report` in this shape:\\nUse empty arrays when no items apply; array fields contain strings unless object entries are shown.\\n`criteriaSatisfied[].status` must be exactly one of: satisfied, not-satisfied, not-applicable.\\n`commandsRun[].result` must be exactly one of: passed, failed, not-run.\\n`manualNotes` and `notes` are optional strings; an empty string means no note and does not satisfy `manual-notes` evidence.\\n```acceptance-repor\n[... omitted ...]\nn    writer: asyncio.StreamWriter,\\n    *,\\n    input_bytes_max: int,\\n    chunk_bytes: int,\\n) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Stream the bounded artifact body to the upload socket and finish with EOF.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    transferred = 0\\n    while True:\\n        try:\\n            content = await artifact_reader.read(chunk_bytes)\\n        except OSError as error:\\n            raise RelayError(\\\"input_unknown\\\") from error\\n        if not content:\\n            break\\n        transferred += len(content)\\n        if transferred > input_bytes_max:\\n            raise RelayError(\\\"input_limit\\\")\\n        writer.write(content)\\n        await writer.drain()\\n    writer.write_eof()\\n\\n\\nasync def _exchange(\\n    control_frame: bytes,\\n    artifact_reader: asyncio.StreamReader,\\n    *,\\n    broker_socket: Path,\\n    connect: BrokerConnect,\\n    input_bytes_max: int,\\n    chunk_bytes: int,\\n) -> bytes:\\n    reader, writer = await connect(broker_socket)\\n    upload_task = asyncio.ensure_future(\\n        _stream_upload(\\n            artifact_reader,\\n            writer,\\n            input_bytes_max=input_bytes_max,\\n            chunk_bytes=chunk_bytes,\\n        )\\n    )\\n    response_task = asyncio.ensure_future(_read_response(reader))\\n    try:\\n        while True:\\n            await asyncio.wait(\\n                {upload_task, response_task},\\n                return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED,\\n            )\\n            if response_task.done() and response_task.exception() is None:\\n                return response_task.result()\\n            if upload_task.done():\\n                upload_task.result()\\n                return await response_task\\n            await upload_task\\n    except BaseException:\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        with suppress(Exception):\\n            writer.transport.abort()\\n        raise\\n    finally:\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        writer.close()\\n        with suppress(OSError):\\n            await writer.wait_closed()\\n```\\n\\nNeed `Any` import from typing — currently typing imports: BinaryIO, TypeAlias, cast. Add Any.\\n\\nWait — issue: `finally` awaits `_settle_discard` — on the success path (`return response_task.result()`), finally cancels the upload (already running) — correct. But the await inside finally during OUR cancellation (outer timeout): `_settle_discard` re-raises our CancelledError from the shield loop → propagates from finally → replaces the return/exception — hmm, if we were cancelled, the try block would have raised CancelledError already (wait/await points) → except BaseException → settle (raises our CancelledError again!) → finally → settle again... The first settle in except re-raises our pending cancellation → finally runs → settle → raises again → abort skipped?? The `with suppress(Exception): writer.transport.abort()` sits in except BEFORE... order in except: settle upload (may raise our CancelledError) → settle response (skipped) → abort (skipped) → raise. Then finally: settle upload → raises our CancelledError again → abort + close skipped. Transport never aborted on cancellation! Old code aborted via except BaseException → abort → raise. The cancellation test (`test_relay_cancellation_aborts_stalled_upload`) asserts the server sees closure (`closed` set) — with abort skipped, the client task ends but the writer remains open → server's `await reader.read()` waits for EOF... connection stays open → closed never sets → test hangs!!\\n\\nWait — but does our CancelledError actually get RE-RAISED by `_settle_discard`'s shield loop? The cancel arrives while suspended at `await asyncio.wait(...)` → CancelledError → except BaseException → `await _settle_discard(upload_task)`: task.cancel() → `await asyncio.shield(task)` → task completes via cancellation quickly → shield raises CancelledError (the task's, because shield propagates child cancellation as CancelledError to awaiter? No — shield: if the CHILD is cancelled, `await shield(child)` raises CancelledError. Yes!). Then check `current.cancelling()` — is OUR task still marked cancelling? Our CancelledError was raised and CAUGHT (except clause) — in Python 3.11, when cancellation is delivered, `task.cancelling()` stays >0 until `uncancel()` is called. So cancelling() > 0 → re-raise. So settle re-raises OUR cancellation before aborting.\\n\\nHmm — but the codebase pattern (e.g., workspace_publication._await_local_effect, broker_artifact_store._run_blocking) uses this exact `current.cancelling()` re-raise pattern inside cleanup loops... In `_run_blocking`, the cancellation was RAISED by shield and re-raised at the END (`raise cancellation`) — after the loop completed. Their loop: `while not task.done(): try: await shield(task) except CancelledError as error: cancellation = error` — they CAPTURE and continue looping, never re-raising mid-cleanup; the task completes (to_thread can't be truly cancelled — it runs to completion), then they raise. For my upload task, cancel() DOES complete it quickly (I/O cancellation) → loop exits after first CancelledError → `task.result()` (suppressed) → return normally! Wait — re-read my `_settle_discard`: on CancelledError we check `current.cancelling()` and re-raise if > 0. Our task IS cancelling (cancelling()>0) → re-raise immediately — WITHOUT waiting for the child task to finish. That's the problem.\\n\\nShould I re-raise? The purpose of the cancelling() check is to not swallow genuine cancellation requests. But here we're in cleanup and the child task needs draining. If we re-raise, the child task stays pending (it was cancelled — it'll finish soon on its own) and the writer never aborts/closes. Options:\\n\\n(a) Don't re-raise; just keep draining until the task is done (the codebase's `_run_blocking` style: capture, continue, raise after). The child finishes fast (cancelled I/O). Then after draining both tasks, re-raise the captured cancellation at the END of cleanup? The except/finally structure makes this awkward.\\n\\n(b) Reorder: in except BaseException → abort FIRST, then settle tasks, then raise. Abort closes the transport immediately → both tasks' I/O fails/cancels fast. Then settle drains them. If our own cancellation arrives mid-settle → cancelling()>0 → re-raise → finally skips remaining settle/close — but transport already aborted; tasks were cancelled and will die with the loop. The `closed`-event in the test relies on abort → happens first ✓.\\n\\nGo with (b): abort first in except. Also — in the CANCELLATION case, old code path: CancelledError → `except BaseException:` → abort → raise. Same.\\n\\nBut also the finally block: after except re-raises (CancelledError), finally runs `_settle_discard(upload_task)` → cancelling()>0 → shield loop → first CancelledError (child's, shield-propagated) → check cancelling()>0 → re-raise → propagates out of finally → same CancelledError → fine (exception unchanged). abort already done. Acceptable.\\n\\nActually, hmm, wait — with abort FIRST, do the settle drains even matter? They retrieve exceptions to avoid warnings and let cancel take effect. Keep them after abort.\\n\\nOne more ordering concern: in the RESPONSE-WINS success path (return inside try), finally settles upload (cancel → drain) THEN closes writer. Good — finding says \\\"Cancel and drain upload safely once response arrives.\\\" ✓.\\n\\nNow — `current.cancelling()` re-raise inside `_settle_discard` when OUR cancellation is pending mid-drain: could that abandon a child mid-drain leaving \\\"never retrieved\\\" warnings? Task was cancelled → its exception is CancelledError → asyncio doesn't warn for cancelled tasks. OK.\\n\\nAlso — `await asyncio.wait(...)` raising CancelledError on outer timeout → except → abort → settle (re-raises our cancellation quickly) → finally settle (re-raise again) → propagate. Relay wrapper maps? relay_artifact_upload's `except asyncio.CancelledError: raise` — passes through; wait_for in CLI converts TimeoutError → RelayError. But direct cancellation of relay_artifact_upload propagates CancelledError ✓ (existing contract).\\n\\nHmm — one more subtle thing: `relay_artifact_upload` wraps `_exchange` in `asyncio.wait_for(..., timeout=timeout_seconds)`. On timeout, wait_for CANCELS _exchange → except BaseException → abort → settle → finally → re-raise CancelledError → wait_for converts to TimeoutError → mapped to RelayError(\\\"timeout_unknown\\\") ✓ (test_transfer timeouts in transport tests rely on this — via service though).\\n\\nOK. Now — another important check: `except BaseException` in _exchange also catches the `RelayError` raised by `upload_task.result()` (input_limit) and by `await response_task` (transport/verification) → abort → settle → re-raise RelayError ✓ (matches old behavior where RelayError from _read_response also hit abort).\\n\\nNow finalize the except block:\\n\\n```python\\n    except BaseException:\\n        with suppress(Exception):\\n            writer.transport.abort()\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        raise\\n```\\n\\nAnd finally keeps settle+close. If settle in except re-raises our cancellation, finally's settle re-raises again — fine.\\n\\nNow — `_settle_discard` re-raise condition: also guard `except BaseException: break` for child failures ✓.\\n\\nType: `task: asyncio.Future[Any]` — import Any.\\n\\nNow the CLI parts. Edit 3 — replace `_run_relay` and `main`:\\n\\noldText:\\n```python\\nasync def _run_relay(control_frame: bytes, source: BinaryIO) -> bytes:\\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\\n    reader = asyncio.StreamReader()\\n    protocol = asyncio.StreamReaderProtocol(reader)\\n    transport, _protocol = await loop.connect_read_pipe(lambda: protocol, source)\\n    try:\\n        return await relay_artifact_upload(\\n            control_frame,\\n            reader,\\n            broker_socket=DEFAULT_ARTIFACT_UPLOAD_SOCKET,\\n            REDACTED,\\n        )\\n    finally:\\n        transport.close()\\n\\n\\ndef main(argv: Sequence[str] | None = None) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Relay a control frame and remaining stdin bytes to the fixed upload socket.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    build_parser().parse_args(argv)\\n    try:\\n        buffered_input = sys.stdin.buffer\\n        source = cast(BinaryIO, getattr(buffered_input, \\\"raw\\\",\",\"thinkingSignature\":\"\"}],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0},\"cacheWrite1h\":0},\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1789298058918,\"responseId\":\"msg_2026091319141958345caaa5de4882\",\"errorMessage\":\"terminated\"}}\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789298366638,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:19:26.638Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"errorMessage\":\"Request timed out.\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"cost\":0},\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1789298356109,\"errorMessage\":\"Request timed out.\"}}\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789298381346,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:19:41.346Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"errorMessage\":\"Request timed out.\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"cost\":0},\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1789298370823,\"errorMessage\":\"Request timed out.\"}}\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789298391185,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:19:51.185Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"errorMessage\":\"Connection error.\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"cost\":0},\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1789298389557,\"errorMessage\":\"Connection error.\"}}\n[Bash output limited. Full output: /var/folders/sp/rk1j_1ws7rqdyg8245gpw5hh0000gn/T/pi-bash-795bc39c06c0bcae.log]"}],"details":{"truncation":{"content":" M backend/src/yinshi/services/broker_artifact_store.py\n?? backend/src/yinshi/broker_artifact_stdio_relay.py\n?? backend/tests/test_broker_artifact_stdio_relay.py\n?? backend/tests/test_broker_artifact_transport.py\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789297411025,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:03:31.025Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"user\",\"text\":\"Task: You are a delegated subagent running from a fork of the parent session. Treat the inherited conversation as reference-only context, not a live thread to continue. Do not continue or answer prior messages as if they are waiting for a reply. Your sole job is to execute the task below and return a focused result for that task using your tools.\\n\\nTask:\\nFix all three findings from final review 5dcf482a in current shared checkout. Do not commit. 1) Relay must concurrently watch signed broker response while streaming body. A complete valid framed response wins over later upload-side BrokenPipe/connection errors. Cancel and drain upload safely once response arrives. If upload finishes first, await response. Add integrated large remaining body early metadata rejection test. 2) BrokerArtifactStore concurrent exact retries: when no-replace publication loses, quarantine losing pending attempt then inspect the winning final against exact manifest. Exact match returns deterministic receipt, conflicting final remains rejection/unresolved per confirmed state. Add synchronized concurrent exact and conflicting manifest tests. Preserve root lock/capacity, cancellation, directory sync, and no mutable deletion. 3) CLI control-frame read must be under one absolute relay timeout. Build async pipe reader first, read length/control and remaining artifacts through the same reader so buffering is preserved. Avoid blocking sys.stdin reads before timeout. Add partial-prefix and partial-control-frame stall tests at the appropriate async boundary. Keep existing one-frame relay untouched. Review current source and tests first. Run focused tests, Ruff, format, strict mypy, py_compile, diff check. If TDD Guard blocks, return exact edit packet. No semicolons.\\n\\n## Acceptance Contract\\nAcceptance level: checked\\nCompletion is not accepted from prose alone. End with a structured acceptance report.\\n\\nCriteria:\\n- criterion-1: Implement the requested change without widening scope\\n\\nRequired evidence: changed-files, tests-added, commands-run, residual-risks, no-staged-files, validation-output\\n\\nFinish with a fenced JSON block tagged `acceptance-report` in this shape:\\nUse empty arrays when no items apply; array fields contain strings unless object entries are shown.\\n`criteriaSatisfied[].status` must be exactly one of: satisfied, not-satisfied, not-applicable.\\n`commandsRun[].result` must be exactly one of: passed, failed, not-run.\\n`manualNotes` and `notes` are optional strings; an empty string means no note and does not satisfy `manual-notes` evidence.\\n```acceptance-repor\n[... omitted ...]\nn    writer: asyncio.StreamWriter,\\n    *,\\n    input_bytes_max: int,\\n    chunk_bytes: int,\\n) -> None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Stream the bounded artifact body to the upload socket and finish with EOF.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    transferred = 0\\n    while True:\\n        try:\\n            content = await artifact_reader.read(chunk_bytes)\\n        except OSError as error:\\n            raise RelayError(\\\"input_unknown\\\") from error\\n        if not content:\\n            break\\n        transferred += len(content)\\n        if transferred > input_bytes_max:\\n            raise RelayError(\\\"input_limit\\\")\\n        writer.write(content)\\n        await writer.drain()\\n    writer.write_eof()\\n\\n\\nasync def _exchange(\\n    control_frame: bytes,\\n    artifact_reader: asyncio.StreamReader,\\n    *,\\n    broker_socket: Path,\\n    connect: BrokerConnect,\\n    input_bytes_max: int,\\n    chunk_bytes: int,\\n) -> bytes:\\n    reader, writer = await connect(broker_socket)\\n    upload_task = asyncio.ensure_future(\\n        _stream_upload(\\n            artifact_reader,\\n            writer,\\n            input_bytes_max=input_bytes_max,\\n            chunk_bytes=chunk_bytes,\\n        )\\n    )\\n    response_task = asyncio.ensure_future(_read_response(reader))\\n    try:\\n        while True:\\n            await asyncio.wait(\\n                {upload_task, response_task},\\n                return_when=asyncio.FIRST_COMPLETED,\\n            )\\n            if response_task.done() and response_task.exception() is None:\\n                return response_task.result()\\n            if upload_task.done():\\n                upload_task.result()\\n                return await response_task\\n            await upload_task\\n    except BaseException:\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        with suppress(Exception):\\n            writer.transport.abort()\\n        raise\\n    finally:\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        writer.close()\\n        with suppress(OSError):\\n            await writer.wait_closed()\\n```\\n\\nNeed `Any` import from typing — currently typing imports: BinaryIO, TypeAlias, cast. Add Any.\\n\\nWait — issue: `finally` awaits `_settle_discard` — on the success path (`return response_task.result()`), finally cancels the upload (already running) — correct. But the await inside finally during OUR cancellation (outer timeout): `_settle_discard` re-raises our CancelledError from the shield loop → propagates from finally → replaces the return/exception — hmm, if we were cancelled, the try block would have raised CancelledError already (wait/await points) → except BaseException → settle (raises our CancelledError again!) → finally → settle again... The first settle in except re-raises our pending cancellation → finally runs → settle → raises again → abort skipped?? The `with suppress(Exception): writer.transport.abort()` sits in except BEFORE... order in except: settle upload (may raise our CancelledError) → settle response (skipped) → abort (skipped) → raise. Then finally: settle upload → raises our CancelledError again → abort + close skipped. Transport never aborted on cancellation! Old code aborted via except BaseException → abort → raise. The cancellation test (`test_relay_cancellation_aborts_stalled_upload`) asserts the server sees closure (`closed` set) — with abort skipped, the client task ends but the writer remains open → server's `await reader.read()` waits for EOF... connection stays open → closed never sets → test hangs!!\\n\\nWait — but does our CancelledError actually get RE-RAISED by `_settle_discard`'s shield loop? The cancel arrives while suspended at `await asyncio.wait(...)` → CancelledError → except BaseException → `await _settle_discard(upload_task)`: task.cancel() → `await asyncio.shield(task)` → task completes via cancellation quickly → shield raises CancelledError (the task's, because shield propagates child cancellation as CancelledError to awaiter? No — shield: if the CHILD is cancelled, `await shield(child)` raises CancelledError. Yes!). Then check `current.cancelling()` — is OUR task still marked cancelling? Our CancelledError was raised and CAUGHT (except clause) — in Python 3.11, when cancellation is delivered, `task.cancelling()` stays >0 until `uncancel()` is called. So cancelling() > 0 → re-raise. So settle re-raises OUR cancellation before aborting.\\n\\nHmm — but the codebase pattern (e.g., workspace_publication._await_local_effect, broker_artifact_store._run_blocking) uses this exact `current.cancelling()` re-raise pattern inside cleanup loops... In `_run_blocking`, the cancellation was RAISED by shield and re-raised at the END (`raise cancellation`) — after the loop completed. Their loop: `while not task.done(): try: await shield(task) except CancelledError as error: cancellation = error` — they CAPTURE and continue looping, never re-raising mid-cleanup; the task completes (to_thread can't be truly cancelled — it runs to completion), then they raise. For my upload task, cancel() DOES complete it quickly (I/O cancellation) → loop exits after first CancelledError → `task.result()` (suppressed) → return normally! Wait — re-read my `_settle_discard`: on CancelledError we check `current.cancelling()` and re-raise if > 0. Our task IS cancelling (cancelling()>0) → re-raise immediately — WITHOUT waiting for the child task to finish. That's the problem.\\n\\nShould I re-raise? The purpose of the cancelling() check is to not swallow genuine cancellation requests. But here we're in cleanup and the child task needs draining. If we re-raise, the child task stays pending (it was cancelled — it'll finish soon on its own) and the writer never aborts/closes. Options:\\n\\n(a) Don't re-raise; just keep draining until the task is done (the codebase's `_run_blocking` style: capture, continue, raise after). The child finishes fast (cancelled I/O). Then after draining both tasks, re-raise the captured cancellation at the END of cleanup? The except/finally structure makes this awkward.\\n\\n(b) Reorder: in except BaseException → abort FIRST, then settle tasks, then raise. Abort closes the transport immediately → both tasks' I/O fails/cancels fast. Then settle drains them. If our own cancellation arrives mid-settle → cancelling()>0 → re-raise → finally skips remaining settle/close — but transport already aborted; tasks were cancelled and will die with the loop. The `closed`-event in the test relies on abort → happens first ✓.\\n\\nGo with (b): abort first in except. Also — in the CANCELLATION case, old code path: CancelledError → `except BaseException:` → abort → raise. Same.\\n\\nBut also the finally block: after except re-raises (CancelledError), finally runs `_settle_discard(upload_task)` → cancelling()>0 → shield loop → first CancelledError (child's, shield-propagated) → check cancelling()>0 → re-raise → propagates out of finally → same CancelledError → fine (exception unchanged). abort already done. Acceptable.\\n\\nActually, hmm, wait — with abort FIRST, do the settle drains even matter? They retrieve exceptions to avoid warnings and let cancel take effect. Keep them after abort.\\n\\nOne more ordering concern: in the RESPONSE-WINS success path (return inside try), finally settles upload (cancel → drain) THEN closes writer. Good — finding says \\\"Cancel and drain upload safely once response arrives.\\\" ✓.\\n\\nNow — `current.cancelling()` re-raise inside `_settle_discard` when OUR cancellation is pending mid-drain: could that abandon a child mid-drain leaving \\\"never retrieved\\\" warnings? Task was cancelled → its exception is CancelledError → asyncio doesn't warn for cancelled tasks. OK.\\n\\nAlso — `await asyncio.wait(...)` raising CancelledError on outer timeout → except → abort → settle (re-raises our cancellation quickly) → finally settle (re-raise again) → propagate. Relay wrapper maps? relay_artifact_upload's `except asyncio.CancelledError: raise` — passes through; wait_for in CLI converts TimeoutError → RelayError. But direct cancellation of relay_artifact_upload propagates CancelledError ✓ (existing contract).\\n\\nHmm — one more subtle thing: `relay_artifact_upload` wraps `_exchange` in `asyncio.wait_for(..., timeout=timeout_seconds)`. On timeout, wait_for CANCELS _exchange → except BaseException → abort → settle → finally → re-raise CancelledError → wait_for converts to TimeoutError → mapped to RelayError(\\\"timeout_unknown\\\") ✓ (test_transfer timeouts in transport tests rely on this — via service though).\\n\\nOK. Now — another important check: `except BaseException` in _exchange also catches the `RelayError` raised by `upload_task.result()` (input_limit) and by `await response_task` (transport/verification) → abort → settle → re-raise RelayError ✓ (matches old behavior where RelayError from _read_response also hit abort).\\n\\nNow finalize the except block:\\n\\n```python\\n    except BaseException:\\n        with suppress(Exception):\\n            writer.transport.abort()\\n        await _settle_discard(upload_task)\\n        await _settle_discard(response_task)\\n        raise\\n```\\n\\nAnd finally keeps settle+close. If settle in except re-raises our cancellation, finally's settle re-raises again — fine.\\n\\nNow — `_settle_discard` re-raise condition: also guard `except BaseException: break` for child failures ✓.\\n\\nType: `task: asyncio.Future[Any]` — import Any.\\n\\nNow the CLI parts. Edit 3 — replace `_run_relay` and `main`:\\n\\noldText:\\n```python\\nasync def _run_relay(control_frame: bytes, source: BinaryIO) -> bytes:\\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\\n    reader = asyncio.StreamReader()\\n    protocol = asyncio.StreamReaderProtocol(reader)\\n    transport, _protocol = await loop.connect_read_pipe(lambda: protocol, source)\\n    try:\\n        return await relay_artifact_upload(\\n            control_frame,\\n            reader,\\n            broker_socket=DEFAULT_ARTIFACT_UPLOAD_SOCKET,\\n            REDACTED,\\n        )\\n    finally:\\n        transport.close()\\n\\n\\ndef main(argv: Sequence[str] | None = None) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Relay a control frame and remaining stdin bytes to the fixed upload socket.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    build_parser().parse_args(argv)\\n    try:\\n        buffered_input = sys.stdin.buffer\\n        source = cast(BinaryIO, getattr(buffered_input, \\\"raw\\\",\",\"thinkingSignature\":\"\"}],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0},\"cacheWrite1h\":0},\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"timestamp\":1789298058918,\"responseId\":\"msg_2026091319141958345caaa5de4882\",\"errorMessage\":\"terminated\"}}\n{\"version\":1,\"recordType\":\"message\",\"source\":\"foreground\",\"runId\":\"265df3dd\",\"agent\":\"worker-advanced\",\"childIndex\":0,\"cwd\":\"/Users/user/projects/yinshi\",\"ts\":1789298366638,\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-13T11:19:26.638Z\",\"sourceEventType\":\"message_end\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"stopReason\":\"error\",\"errorMessage\":\"Request timed out.\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"cost\":0},\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[],\"api\":\"anthropic-messages\",\"provider\":\"z-ai\",\"model\":\"glm-5.3-flash\",\"usage\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"totalTokens\":0,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\